%% The Topic 7 revision sheet — included by KB Part Five and by the RN volume. Must make sense on its own.

## 1. Defining gender-based violence (2017, 2023, 2024)

- **Violence** (WHO **2002**) — intentional use of physical force or **power**, threatened or actual, causing or likely to cause injury, death, psychological harm, maldevelopment or **deprivation**.
- **Gender-based violence (GBV)** — harm directed at a person **because of their gender**, rooted in **gender inequality, abuse of power and harmful norms** (UNHCR, UN Women); physical, sexual, psychological, economic; threats included; public or private life. MCQ 2025: violence based on **sex or gender**. Falls mainly on women and girls; can also target men, boys and sexual minorities.
- **DEVAW** (UN General Assembly, **20 Dec 1993**) — **Art. 1**: "any act of gender-based violence that results in, or is likely to result in, physical, sexual or psychological harm or suffering to women, including threats of such acts, coercion or arbitrary deprivation of liberty, whether occurring in public or in private life". **Art. 2**: three sites — **family** (battering, sexual abuse of girls, dowry violence, marital rape, harmful traditional practices), **community** (rape, harassment at work and in schools, trafficking, forced prostitution), **State** (perpetrated or condoned).
- **Milestones** — CEDAW **1979** (silent on violence) → **GR 19 (1992)**: GBV is **discrimination** → **Vienna 1993** ("women's rights are human rights") → DEVAW 1993 → UN **Special Rapporteur** and **Belém do Pará** (1994) → **Beijing 1995, Critical Area D** → **25 November** designated **1999**; **16 Days of Activism** (25 Nov–10 Dec) → UNSCR **1325** (2000) → **Istanbul Convention 2011** (prevention, protection, prosecution, integrated policies) → **SDG 5.2** (end violence) and **5.3** (end child marriage, 2015) → **GR 35 (2017)**.
- **Scale** — WHO **2021**: about **1 in 3** women (≈736 million) experience physical or sexual violence (MCQ 2024). **UNODC/UN Women 2024**: **85,000** women and girls killed intentionally in **2023**; **51,100** (60%) by partners or family — **140 a day, one every 10 minutes**.
- **Pakistan** — **PDHS 2017–18**: **28%** of ever-married women physical violence since 15; **6%** sexual; **34%** spousal physical, sexual or emotional; **56%** never sought help. **SSDO 2024**: **32,617** GBV cases (kidnapping 24,439; rape 5,339; domestic violence 2,238; honour killing 547); conviction ≈ **0.5%** rape and honour, 1.3% DV, 0.1% kidnapping. **HRCP 2024**: **405** honour killings; 3,300+ beatings; 14,425 cases documented.
- **Punjab Act 2016, s. 2(r)** — "violence" = any offence against the body of the aggrieved person incl. **abetment**, **domestic violence, sexual violence, psychological abuse, economic abuse, stalking, cybercrime**.
- **Classify GBV** — by **nature** (physical, sexual, psychological, economic); by **site** (home, community/workplace, public spaces, online, state/custody); by **perpetrator** (partner, family, acquaintance, stranger, state); by **life cycle** (sex selection → neglect of girls, child abuse → child marriage, *vani*, harassment → DV, marital rape, honour killing, acid, dowry → neglect of widows, denial of inheritance).
- **Continuum** — **Liz Kelly** (*Surviving Sexual Violence*, **1988**): everyday harassment to rape and femicide are connected by men's power and women's fear.
- **Why it matters** — human rights (Arts **9, 14, 25**), public health ("**epidemic proportions**", WHO), development (SDG 5; costs ≈ **2%** of world GDP), democracy (fear keeps women out of public life).
- **GBV or VAW?** — define GBV; explain it falls mainly on women; use DEVAW for VAW.

## 2. Theories of violence against women (2020, 2025)

- **Biological** — testosterone, evolution, mate-guarding. *Critique*: ignores variation across countries and time; most men non-violent; abusers control violence elsewhere; excuses violence.
- **Psychological** — personality, anger, mental illness, alcohol and drugs, childhood trauma. *Critique*: most abusers not ill; violence planned and selective; alcohol a trigger, not cause; victim-blaming ("provocation").
- **Social learning** — **Albert Bandura**: **Bobo doll** experiments (**1961**), *Aggression: A Social Learning Analysis* (**1973**); violence learned by **observing** and when **rewarded/unpunished**; **intergenerational transmission** (sons copy fathers, daughters accept); media models. MCQs: domestic violence is **learned behaviour** (2023); violence is **socially constructed** (2024). *Critique*: many from violent homes are not violent; does not explain why men target women. PDHS: large shares of women and men say wife-beating justified for some reason.
- **Resource and exchange** — **William Goode (1971)**: force is a resource used when others (money, status) fail; **exchange**: violence when rewards exceed costs (no police, no sanction). Explains job loss, wives earning more, low costs. *Critique*: rich men abuse; does not explain male entitlement.
- **Feminist** — **Dobash and Dobash**, *Violence Against Wives* (**1979**): wife-beating = expression of male authority and entitlement, supported by law, religion, custom; **patriarchy** the root; violence **functional** (keeps women subordinate); "the personal is political"; institutions complicit. **Susan Brownmiller**, *Against Our Will* (**1975**): rape keeps "all women in a state of fear" — power, not sex. *Critique*: may overlook women's violence, same-sex violence, class and race; **Crenshaw**, "Mapping the Margins" (**1991**) — intersectionality.
- **Power and control** — **Duluth** wheel (Domestic Abuse Intervention Project, **1981**; **Ellen Pence**, **Michael Paymar**): hub **power and control**; spokes **intimidation, emotional abuse, isolation, minimising/denying/blaming, using children, male privilege, economic abuse, coercion and threats**; rim **physical and sexual violence**; "co-ordinated community response".
- **Cycle of violence** — **Lenore Walker**, *The Battered Woman* (**1979**): **tension building → acute battering → loving contrition ("honeymoon")** → repeat; explains why women stay; "**learned helplessness**" (criticised).
- **Coercive control** — **Evan Stark** (**2007**): pattern of isolation, intimidation, surveillance, degradation; like a hostage; crime in England and Wales (2015). **Michael Johnson** (1995, 2008): **intimate terrorism** (control, mostly men) vs **situational couple violence** (both sexes, less severe).
- **Ecological model** — **Lori Heise (1998)**, adopted by WHO: **individual** (witnessed violence, alcohol, low education) → **relationship** (male control of money and decisions, conflict, dowry) → **community** (poverty, *jirgas*, isolation, peer approval) → **society** (male entitlement, honour norms, acceptance of violence, impunity).
- **Galtung** — **direct** (visible, a perpetrator), **structural** (**1969**: built into structures; "social injustice"; potential below actual), **cultural** (**1990**: beliefs that legitimise the other two).
- **Line** — ecological, feminist approach: patriarchal norms and impunity (society), *jirgas* (community), learned behaviour and economic stress (relationship, individual).

## 3. Power and control (2020)

- **Instrumental violence** — not just anger; a way to get and keep control over movement, money, friends, sexuality, marriage, thoughts; the **threat** governs life even without frequent beatings.
- **Pakistani tactics** — intimidation (threats of *talaq* or second marriage; smashing goods); emotional abuse (taunts about dowry, sons, her family); isolation (no visits to parents, phone taken, purdah as control); minimising/blaming ("a husband may discipline"); using children (custody threats); male privilege (husband and in-laws decide work, school, health, contraception); economic abuse (salary or dowry taken, inheritance denied, no paid work); coercion and threats (acid, honour killing, harm to family).
- **Punjab Act 2016** recognises economic and psychological abuse, stalking and cybercrime — not only assault.
- **Commenting** — strengths: explains selective, patterned, gendered violence; non-physical abuse is real; fits honour killing, acid, forced marriage (punishing choice); guides practice (Duluth model, perpetrator programmes). Limits: focus on intimate partners; may underplay alcohol, illness, poverty; mixed results of programmes; misses situational violence. **Line**: best single explanation, inside an ecological frame.

## 4. Structural and direct violence; Mukhtaran Mai (2019)

- **Direct** — beating, rape and gang rape, acid, honour killing, stove burning, harassment, forced marriage, abduction; 32,617 reported cases (2024).
- **Structural** — female literacy ≈**53%**; MMR **186**; LFPR **24.4%**; denial of inheritance; laws that let honour killers be pardoned (before 2016); **Hudood 1979** (rape victims risked *zina* charges); slow courts, ≈0.5% convictions; *jirgas*; police refusal.
- **Cultural** — honour (*izzat*, *ghairat*) in women's bodies; husband may "discipline"; daughters as *paraya dhan*; misused religion; dramas romanticising control.
- **Vicious circle** — cultural beliefs justify structural arrangements → vulnerability to direct violence → impunity confirms beliefs.
- **Mukhtaran Mai** — direct (gang rape on *panchayat* order), structural (clan hierarchy, parallel justice, slow courts, acquittals, poverty), cultural (woman as honour debt; silence or suicide expected; victim-blaming).

## 5. Root causes and how they intersect (2025)

- **Root cause vs trigger** — root causes (patriarchy, inequality, impunity) explain why violence is common and gendered; triggers (dowry quarrel, unemployment, drugs, a daughter's choice) explain when.
- **Patriarchy** — male authority and guardianship; honour; son preference; control of sexuality and marriage (forced marriage, *watta satta*); ***Saima Waheed*** (SC **2004**): adult woman may marry without *wali*.
- **Economic dependence** — LFPR 24.4%; little land or inheritance (Property Rights Act **2020**); cannot leave; poverty, unemployment and dowry raise tensions (resource theory).
- **Impunity** — SSDO 2024: rape 0.5%, honour 0.5%, DV 1.3%, kidnapping 0.1%; Punjab **225** honour killings, **2** convictions; Sindh **0** convictions for rape and honour; FIR refusal; poor forensics; witness pressure; slow trials; pardons (Qandeel Baloch acquittal **2022**).
- **Customs and parallel justice** — *jirgas* and *panchayats* order *vani*, *swara*, *kari* killings, punishment rape (Mukhtaran Mai); Supreme Court declared rights-violating *jirgas* illegal; Anti-Women Practices Act **2011**.
- **Ideas** — low literacy and awareness; misinterpreted religion (mainstream scholars reject honour killing); media stereotypes; social media triggers.
- **Crisis** — militancy (Malala 2012; schools destroyed), displacement, **2022 floods**, **COVID-19** lockdowns (2020).
- **Intersection of causes** — norms × economy (honour keeps women home; dependence keeps them silent); economy × law (poor women cannot afford justice; rich abusers buy settlements); law × custom (where courts fail, *jirgas* rule); ideas × institutions (police see "family matters" as private).
- **Intersection of identities** — rural and poor women; **Hindu and Christian girls** (abduction, forced conversion and marriage, Sindh and Punjab); women with disabilities; child **domestic workers**; ***khawaja siras*** (despite the Transgender Act **2018**).
- **Ecological levels** — society, community, relationship, individual; strategies at all four.
- **Line** — patriarchy is the root; **impunity** and **economic dependence** keep it producing violence; attack norms, independence and justice together.

## 6. Sites and forms in Pakistan (2016, 2017, 2023)

- **Sites** — **home** (most dangerous: DV, marital rape, honour killing, dowry, stove burning); **community** (*jirgas*, *vani*/*swara*, rape, acid, abduction); **workplace** (harassment; domestic and home-based workers); **public spaces and transport** (street harassment, stalking); **online** (cyber-harassment, blackmail, image abuse); **state and custody** (custodial abuse, police inaction, discriminatory laws).
- **Domestic violence** — beating, burning, starvation, marital rape, threats, confinement, economic abuse; **dowry** violence (Dowry and Bridal Gifts Act **1976** unenforced); **stove burning**; DV laws Sindh **2013**, Balochistan **2014**, Punjab **2016**, KP **2021**, ICT **2025**.
- **Rape** — 5,339 cases (2024), 0.5% conviction; gang rape as punishment; **Zainab Ansari** (Kasur, Jan **2018**) → **Zainab Alert Act 2020**; **motorway gang rape** (Sept **2020**; victim-blaming by a senior officer) → **Anti-Rape Act 2021**; Zina Ordinance 1979 → **2006 Act** → **2016** DNA amendment.
- **Acid attacks** — revenge for refused proposals or advances, family disputes; blindness, disfigurement, isolation; **Acid Act 2011** (ss. **336A, 336B**; **14 years to life**, fine ≥ **Rs 1 million**); *Saving Face* (2012).
- **Harassment** — workplace (**2010** Act; inquiry committees, code of conduct, ombudspersons; amended **2022**); public (**s. 509 PPC**, 2010); education; MCQ 2023.
- **Custom-based** — *vani*/*swara* (**s. 310A**), denial of inheritance (**498A**), forced marriage (**498B**), marriage to the Qur'an (**498C**) — Anti-Women Practices Act **2011**; *watta satta*; child marriage (1929 Act; Sindh **2013** and ICT **2025** = 18).
- **Forced conversion** of Hindu and Christian girls — abduction, conversion, marriage to older men; no national law yet.
- **Trafficking and abduction** — 24,439 kidnapping cases (2024; some filed against daughters who marry by choice); **Trafficking in Persons Act 2018**.
- **Cyber violence** — **PECA 2016** (ss. **20–21** dignity and modesty; **24** cyber-stalking); Digital Rights Foundation helpline (**2016**); Qandeel Baloch (**2016**); **Sana Yousaf** (17, Islamabad, **2 June 2025**, after refusing advances).
- **State violence** — custodial abuse, police refusal, discriminatory laws (Hudood; old *Qisas* and *Diyat* pardons).
- **Answering** — group by site or nature; data for three forms; a law for each; two or three cases.

## 7. Honour killing (2017 note)

- **Definition** — murder of a woman (sometimes a man) by family or community claiming she brought shame; "offences": choosing a husband, divorce, relationships, being raped, talking to a man, refusing marriage, "immodesty", social media.
- **Names** — ***karo-kari*** (Sindh), ***siyahkari*** (Balochistan), ***tor tora*** (KP), ***kala kali*** (Punjab).
- **Scale** — HRCP **405** (2024); SSDO **547** (2024), ≈0.5% convictions; Punjab 225 → 2 convictions; hidden as suicide or accident.
- **Causes** — honour and patriarchy (cultural violence); control of marriage and property; false *karo-kari* accusations to grab land or settle disputes; community pressure (*be-ghairat*); *jirga* approval; impunity; misused religion (CII and scholars condemn).
- **Law** — PPC **1860** "grave and sudden provocation"; ***Qisas and Diyat* Ordinance 1990** (Act **1997**): murder compoundable; heirs (often the killers' family) forgive; **2004 Criminal Law (Amendment) Act**: honour crimes defined, penalties raised, loophole open; **2016 Criminal Law (Amendment) (Offences in the name or pretext of Honour) Act** (joint sitting, **October 2016**): heirs may forgive *qisas* but court must award **life imprisonment** (25 years) as *ta'zir* — MCQ 2026; bill moved by **Sughra Imam** (2014). Weakness: motive must be proved; re-labelling; hostile witnesses.
- **Cases** — **Samia Sarwar** (**1999**, shot in lawyers' office — Hina Jilani and Asma Jahangir — mother present; family unpunished); **Qandeel Baloch** (strangled by brother Waseem, Multan, **July 2016**; life **2019**; LHC acquittal **Feb 2022** after parental pardon); **Saba Qaiser** (19, Gujranwala, **2014**, shot by father and uncle, survived, "forgave" under pressure; *A Girl in the River*); **Bano Satakzai and Ehsanullah Samalani** (Degari, Balochistan, **June 2025**, tribal chief's orders; viral video **July 2025**; arrests).
- **Strategies** — fully non-compoundable; investigate every suspicious death; prosecute *jirgas*; shelters and protection for couples; scholars' statements; education and media; economic independence.
- **Assessment** — 2016 law necessary; impunity continues through settlements, weak investigation and social approval.

## 8. Laws, the Punjab Act 2016 and government initiatives (2018, 2024)

- **Framework** — Arts **9, 14, 25, 35, 37**; CEDAW (**1996**), GR 19 and 35; Beijing Area D; ICCPR and CAT (**2010**); SDG 5.2/5.3; **GSP+** (2014; 27 conventions).
- **Main laws** — **2006** Protection of Women Act (rape to PPC; end of four-witness rule; MCQ 2016); **2010** workplace harassment (+ s. 509); **2011** Acid; **2011** Anti-Women Practices; **2013–2025** provincial DV laws; **2016** honour; **2016** anti-rape (DNA, in-camera trials, penalties for obstructing police); **2016** PECA; **2018** Trafficking; **2020** Zainab Alert; **2020** Property Rights; **2021** Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act (assent **1 Dec 2021**; **Special Sexual Offences Investigation Units**, **Anti-Rape Crisis Cells**, special courts, in-camera, ≈**4 months**, sex-offenders register); **2022** harassment amendment; **2025** ICT DV Act (Sharmila Farooqui; NA 13 Nov, Senate 28 Nov) and ICT child-marriage law (18).
- **Punjab Act 2016** — passed **24 Feb 2016**, gazetted 29 Feb; aim: "**protection, relief and rehabilitation**"; mainly civil. **Forms (s. 2(r))**: abetment, **domestic violence** (by a person she lives/lived with, related by blood, marriage or adoption), **sexual violence**, **psychological abuse** (oppressive behaviour or restricted movement causing depression, anorexia or suicide attempt, certified by a panel), **economic abuse** (denial of food, clothing, shelter; taking her income), **stalking**, **cybercrime**. **Procedure**: Family Court; first hearing within **7 days**; decision within **90 days**. **Orders**: **interim** (s. 6); **protection** (s. 7: no contact, stay away, distance, **GPS bracelet** for grave violence, move out, surrender weapons); **residence** (s. 8: no eviction, stay in house or shelter, rent for alternative accommodation); **monetary** (s. 9: compensation for economic abuse, lost earnings, medical costs, damage, maintenance). **System**: toll-free helpline (s. 3); **District Women Protection Committees** (s. 11); **Protection Centres = Violence against Women Centres** (s. 13: one-stop — FIR, medical, forensics within 24 hours, legal aid, counselling, mediation; first **Multan 2017**); shelter homes; **Women Protection Officers** (s. 14). **Penalties**: breach up to **1 year**, Rs 50,000–200,000; repeat **1–2 years**; false complaint up to **3 months** or Rs 50,000–100,000. **Reaction**: praised as first to recognise economic and psychological abuse, stalking, cybercrime; CII and religious parties called it un-Islamic; mediation risks; slow implementation.
- **Institutions** — **NCSW 2000** (Act 2012); provincial commissions; NCHR 2012; harassment ombudspersons (2010); **women's police stations (1994)**; gender desks; **VAWC Multan (2017)**; **Punjab Women Protection Authority (2017)**; Anti-Rape Crisis Cells; ***Dar-ul-Aman*** shelters; helplines **1043** (Punjab women), **1099** (human rights), **15** (police); family courts; GBV courts (Punjab pilot 2017); special courts (2021); National Policy 2002; BISP 2008.
- **Enforcement gap** — low awareness; family pressure; FIR refusal; poor investigation and forensics; slow courts, hostile witnesses; ≈0.5% convictions; few shelters; uneven provinces after the **18th Amendment**.

## 9. Consequences (2024)

- **Physical** — injury, disability, acid disfigurement, death (51,100 partner/family femicides worldwide, 2023).
- **Sexual and reproductive** — unwanted pregnancy, unsafe abortion, miscarriage, low birth weight, STIs/HIV, fistula, maternal death.
- **Mental** — depression (almost **2×** risk, WHO), anxiety, PTSD, low self-esteem, substance use, suicide; Punjab Act's psychological violence.
- **Economic** — lost work and income, forbidden work, fear of harassment cuts participation (LFPR 24.4%), poverty after leaving.
- **Children** — trauma, poor schooling, intergenerational cycle; child marriage ends schooling.
- **Society and development** — fear limits public life and politics; costs ≈ **2%** of world GDP; weak rule of law; SDGs 3, 4, 5, 8, 16.
- **Silence** — 56% never sought help (PDHS).

## 10. Strategies to eliminate GBV (2016, 2017, 2023)

- **Principles** — attack root causes; all ecological levels; survivor-centred; realistic.
- **Framework** — Istanbul's "four Ps" (prevention, protection, prosecution, policies) → Pakistani "**five Ps**": **Prevention, Protection, Prosecution, Provision, Partnership**.
- **Prevention** — gender equality and consent in schools; keep girls in school; engage men and boys (**Rozan**); religious scholars and *khutbas*; responsible media; women's economic empowerment (jobs, inheritance, BISP); 18-year marriage age and registration.
- **Protection** — 24-hour helplines (1043, 1099, DRF); enforce protection orders; dignified shelters; one-stop centres in every district; safe transport, lighting, CCTV, safe cities; protect couples and witnesses.
- **Prosecution** — compulsory FIRs and action against police who refuse; more women police; training; DNA labs and women medico-legal officers; special courts (2021 Act), fast in-camera trials; non-compoundable GBV crimes; act against *jirgas*.
- **Provision** — free legal aid, medical care, counselling, vocational training, economic support; local languages; rural, minority and disabled women.
- **Partnership** — co-ordinated community response (Duluth); national GBV database; funding; NGOs (**Aurat Foundation, Shirkat Gah, Rozan, War Against Rape, Acid Survivors Foundation**); provincial harmonisation.
- **By level** — society (laws, campaigns, scholars, economic rights); community (*jirgas*, mobilisation, transport, LHWs as first responders); relationship (registration, counselling, women's control of income); individual (girls' schooling, boys' life skills, perpetrator programmes, survivor support).
- **Workable** — quick wins (FIRs, special courts, helplines, centres) + long-term prevention (norms, masculinity).

## 11. Masculinities and violence (2026 note)

- **Masculinity studies** — how manhood is socially constructed and ranked; grew in the **1980s**; **masculinities**, not one masculinity.
- **Connell** (*Gender and Power* **1987**; *Masculinities* **1995**) — **hegemonic** masculinity (honoured ideal legitimising male dominance), **complicit**, **marginalised**, **subordinated**; **patriarchal dividend**.
- **How it explains violence** — proof of manhood; ***ghairat*** before other men (honour killing); **entitlement** (acid attacks on women who refuse; Sana Yousaf **2025**); emotional restriction (anger the only permitted emotion); "crisis of masculinity" under unemployment (resource theory); peer groups, *jirgas*, police, media.
- **Kaufman** (**1987**) — **triad**: violence against women, other men, themselves. **Kimmel** — "**aggrieved entitlement**" (*Guyland* 2008; *Angry White Men* 2013).
- **Pakistan** — provider/protector ideal; sons free, daughters obedient; possessive heroes in dramas; marginalised men assert power at home; *khawaja siras* and "effeminate" men targeted.
- **Role** — focuses on men; violence learned and changeable; explains honour violence and male silence; points to solutions.
- **Practice** — **Rozan** (1998), **MenEngage**, **White Ribbon** (1991), **HeForShe** (2014), scholars modelling non-violence, fathers like **Ziauddin Yousafzai**.
- **Limits** — risk of diverting resources; mixed programme results; must not excuse violence; adapt Western concepts. **Line**: essential, but complements women-centred protection.

## 12. Case study I — Mukhtaran Mai (2019)

- **Background** — Mukhtaran Bibi ("Mukhtar Mai"), **Tatla** (Gujjar) clan, **Meerwala**, **Muzaffargarh**, **South Punjab** (MCQ 2016); powerful **Mastoi** (Baloch) clan; uneducated, taught the Qur'an.
- **Event** — **22 June 2002**: brother **Shakoor** (≈12) accused of a relationship with a Mastoi woman; Mastoi-dominated *panchayat* ordered **gang rape** of Mukhtaran Mai as "punishment"; raped by four men; made to walk home publicly.
- **Breaking silence** — refused silence or suicide; imam's sermon, local journalist, national and international media; case registered.
- **Legal journey** — **ATC D.G. Khan, Aug 2002**: 6 sentenced to death, 8 acquitted; **LHC Multan bench, 2005**: 5 acquitted, **Abdul Khaliq** to life; **Supreme Court, 21 April 2011**: acquittals upheld; review later dismissed.
- **Activism** — compensation (≈Rs 500,000) → **Mukhtar Mai Girls' Model School** (village's first) and boys' school; **Mukhtar Mai Women's Welfare Organisation** (shelter, legal aid, helpline); *Glamour* Woman of the Year (**2005**); memoir ***In the Name of Honor*** (**2006**); married police constable **Nasir Abbas Gabol** (**2009**).
- **State response** — Exit Control List (**2005**), reversed; Musharraf's remark on women "crying rape" for visas — outrage; state worried about its image.
- **Significance** — exposed *panchayat* "justice"; fed the debate behind the **2006 Act**; global symbol; education as resistance. **Evaluation**: courage vs structural injustice.

## 13. Case study II — Malala Yousafzai (2025)

- **Background** — born **12 July 1997**, **Mingora**, Swat (MCQ 2016); father **Ziauddin Yousafzai**, teacher, **Khushal Public School**; named after **Malalai of Maiwand**.
- **Taliban in Swat** — TTP under **Maulana Fazlullah** from **2007**; FM radio edicts; **Dec 2008** order to close girls' schools from Jan 2009; 100+ girls' schools destroyed; military operation **2009**.
- **Early activism** — **BBC Urdu** diary as "**Gul Makai**" (**Jan 2009**, age 11); NYT documentary *Class Dismissed* (2009); **National Youth Peace Prize** (**2011**); International Children's Peace Prize nomination (2011).
- **Attack** — **9 October 2012**, school bus, "Who is Malala?", shot in the head; **Shazia Ramzan** and **Kainat Riaz** wounded; TTP claimed; treated in Peshawar, Rawalpindi and **Birmingham**.
- **Global activism** — UN Youth Assembly, **12 July 2013** ("**Malala Day**"; "One child, one teacher, one book and one pen can change the world"); **Malala Fund** (**2013**, with her father; Education Champions/**Gulmakai Network**; Pakistan, Afghanistan, Nigeria, India, Brazil, Ethiopia, Lebanon; ≈**$14.6m** in Pakistan by 2025); ***I Am Malala*** (**2013**, with Christina Lamb); *We Are Displaced* (2019); **Sakharov Prize** (2013); **Nobel Peace Prize** **10 Dec 2014** with **Kailash Satyarthi** — youngest at **17**; UN Messenger of Peace (2017); Oxford PPE (graduated **2020**).
- **Local** — Shangla school (Nobel money); grants to Pakistani groups; budget advocacy; Art. 25A; **Islamabad, Jan 2025**: Muslim World League "Girls' Education in Muslim Communities" — Afghan girls, "**gender apartheid**", **Islamabad Declaration**.
- **Global context** — SDG 4 (2015); Afghanistan since **2021**; refugee girls.
- **Criticism** — "Western agent"; conspiracy theories; **"I am not Malala" day** (All Pakistan Private Schools Federation, **2014**); selective attention (drone victims); lives abroad.
- **Evaluation** — greatest impact global and symbolic; local impact real but modest beside **25 million+** out-of-school children; individual courage needs state action.

## 14. Case study III — Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy (2020)

- **Profile** — born **1978**, **Karachi**; Smith College, Stanford; journalist turned documentary filmmaker (MCQ 2025); **SOC Films**; first Pakistani Oscar winner — **two Oscars**; *Terror's Children* (2002); Emmy for *Pakistan: Children of the Taliban* (2010); *3 Bahadur* (2015); *Ms. Marvel* episodes (2022); **Hilal-i-Imtiaz** and **TIME 100** (2012).
- ***Saving Face* (Oscar, Feb 2012)** — background: acid attacks, cheap acid (cotton areas of southern Punjab), revenge for refused proposals, impunity; co-director **Daniel Junge**; surgeon **Dr Mohammad Jawad**; survivors **Zakia** (attacked by husband; seeks justice) and **Rukhsana** (attacked by husband and in-laws; returns to live with them); linked to the **Acid Act 2011**.
- ***A Girl in the River: The Price of Forgiveness* (Oscar, Feb 2016)** — background: honour killings, *Qisas* and *Diyat* forgiveness loophole, stalled 2014 bill; **Saba Qaiser**, 19, near **Gujranwala**, married by choice (**2014**), shot by **father and uncle**, thrown into a river in a sack, survived; forgave under elders' pressure; father unrepentant; PM **Nawaz Sharif**: "**There is no honour in honour killing**"; after **Qandeel Baloch** (July 2016) → **October 2016** law; Emmy.
- **Themes** — violence as social problem; impunity; survivors' agency; film as advocacy and law reform; Pakistan's cruelty and its reformers.
- **Criticism** — "washing dirty linen in public"; victim-only portrayal. **Defence** — silence protects abusers; films show reformers and survivors; laws changed.
- **Evaluation** — media turns hidden crimes into national debates; enforcement and social change must follow.
- **The three compared** — Mukhtaran Mai (survivor; *panchayat* rape; schools; 2006 debate; "image" worries; courage vs structural injustice); Malala (activist; Taliban attack; UN, Fund, Nobel; Art. 25A; "Western agent"; education as resistance); Sharmeen (filmmaker; acid and honour; Oscars; 2011 and 2016 laws; "defaming Pakistan"; media for change).

## 15. Debates in one line each

- **More laws or enforcement?** — close specific gaps (marital rape, forced conversion, age 16), but enforcement first.
- **Mediation** — only with free consent, never for serious violence, with safety checks.
- **Forgiveness loophole** — make all GBV murders non-compoundable; train courts to see honour motives.
- **"Un-Islamic" objections** — frame protection in Islamic and constitutional terms; work with scholars.
- **Engaging men** — protect women now, change men for the future.
- **"Image" of Pakistan** — the problem is the violence, not its exposure.
- **Social media** — enforce PECA against abusers; protect women's online freedom; viral exposure can force action.
